#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""talkstick: push-to-talk from an M5StickS3 (or anything speaking M5R1) to a command, a webhook or stdout.

Usage: talkstick serve [--dry]
       talkstick bench WAV
       talkstick fake-stick (WAV | --status | --ping | --watch) [HOST[:PORT]]
       talkstick status
       talkstick ota IMAGE.bin [--version V] [--timeout S]
       talkstick build-sink
Config: JSON at $TALKSTICK_CONFIG, else ~/.config/talkstick/config.json. See config.example.json.

Wire protocol M5R1 (docs/protocol.md; one TCP connection per utterance, ping, watch or fetch):
  stick sends  "M5R1 <device-id> <battery%> <fw-version>\\n", then frames of
               1 byte type + 2 bytes little-endian length + payload:
               A = audio (s16le PCM, 16 kHz, mono, up to 4096 bytes), E = end of utterance, C = cancel,
               S = status request (runs config status_prompt through delivery instead of audio); E and S may
               carry the stick's 8-hex utterance id, and a resent id is never delivered twice,
               P = ping (daemon answers TIME and LAST, then closes), W = watch (connection stays open),
               K <id> = ack an ANSWER, F <version> = fetch the staged firmware image,
               L <text> = the stick's diagnostic lines, written to the event log.
  daemon sends UTF-8 lines: TIME <epoch> <utc-offset-s> (right after HELLO), RECV, ID <8 hex>, WAIT <s>,
               SENT [first 40 chars], REPLY <text> (just before REPLIED), REPLIED, OFFLINE, ERR <reason>,
               LAST <text> (on ping, the last reply). REPLY and LAST text: at most stick_reply_chars (600)
               characters, newlines written as the two characters \\n.
  on a watch   TIME every 5 s, QUEUE <waiting> <busy> <ahead> <paused>, ANSWER <id> <outcome> <said>\\t<text>,
               OTA <size> <sha256> <mac> <version>. On F: IMAGE <size> <sha256>\\n + the raw image, or ERR no image.
  Utterances are delivered in the order their E or S arrived, and the next one is delivered without waiting for
  the previous reply.

Delivery contract
  print    The transcript is printed to the daemon's stdout. The stick sees SENT, then REPLIED.
  command  argv runs with the transcript on stdin (stdin is then closed). Env: TALKSTICK_DEVICE,
           TALKSTICK_UTTERANCE_ID, TALKSTICK_DRY ("1" in dry mode; the command must then not deliver).
           TALKSTICK_SPOKEN_AT (epoch seconds when the button was released).
           A stdout line "@sent" means the text was delivered: the stick sees SENT right away. No "@sent"
           within delivery.command.handoff_timeout seconds (default 45; null = no limit) kills the command and
           the utterance ends in ERR "not posted: ...", so the stick may resend it.
           After "@sent" and before the reply, the status lines "@busy" (the target is running a turn that is
           not this message's), "@paused" (this message is queued and the target's queue is paused) and
           "@running" (this message's turn is running) set the stick's QUEUE; they are never reply text.
           Everything else on stdout is the reply. Exit 0 = REPLIED, exit 3 = target offline (OFFLINE), any other
           exit = ERR with the last stderr line as the reason. No "@sent" before exit 0 means SENT is sent at
           exit.
  webhook  POST url with JSON {"text", "device", "utterance_id", "dry"}. A JSON body {"reply": "..."}
           or a plain-text body is the reply. 503 = offline. Other non-2xx = ERR.
  on_reply Optional argv run with the reply on stdin, trimmed to reply_max_chars plus reply_more_suffix.
           In dry mode it is printed, not run. offline_message, if set, goes through on_reply on OFFLINE.
           on_reply_when "unacked" runs it only when the stick has not acked the answer (K) within
           on_reply_grace seconds; "always" (default) runs it for every reply.
"""
